course: everyday
lesson: 399
title: Left out without a console
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every lunchtime the table talked about one thing: the new game. Zara's family could not afford any console, so she said she «just didn't like games» and stared at her sandwich. Then Ruby asked Zara to explain her favourite board game. Zara explained it so well that half the table played it at break, and by Friday two boys were arguing about its rules instead of the game's. Not having something is not the same as being boring.
explain: Kai's parents said no console at home until he was twelve. Kai declared it the most unfair rule in the history of the world. Then, with all that spare time, he got so good at drawing and football that the class asked him to design the team badge. Ollie had no phone, so he was not in the class group chat, and every Monday he heard everyone laughing at a joke he had not seen. Priya started telling him the funniest bits at break, and he laughed louder than anyone.
explain: Theo told Zara, «You can't play with us, you don't even know the game.» Mia said everyone was new once and taught Zara in five minutes. Noah, scared of being left out, lied that he had the game at home, then went red when Jayden asked which level he was on. He told Jayden the truth. Jayden said «that's fine» and invited him over to play it for real.
explain: Mia's sofa fitted five, and the sixth kid always ended up on the floor. So they piled cushions on the floor and called it the VIP section, and suddenly everyone wanted it. Ruby could only play on Saturdays, so Mia saved the best challenge for Saturdays. Leo's console was his uncle's old one, and the kids laughed at the blocky graphics, until they found the old two-player games were hilarious and started asking to come round for «the retro ones».
explain: One Wednesday Jayden saw Zara watching from the side, quiet. He handed her his controller for a whole match. Zara felt seen, and a bit shy. She lost badly, laughed a lot, and asked to come again. Being invited mattered more to her than winning.
fact: Many families cannot afford a console, or choose not to have one, and that is nothing to be ashamed of.
fact: Asking a left-out friend what they like, and doing it sometimes, brings them back into the group.
fact: Not everyone sees what happens in group chats, so telling friends the good bits in person keeps them included.
fact: Everyone who is good at a game was once a beginner.
fact: Teaching a newcomer is kinder than turning them away.
fact: A small lie to fit in can make you feel more alone, not less.
fact: How you name a place, like calling the floor the VIP section, can change how it feels to sit there.
fact: Good friends plan big moments for when everyone can come.
fact: Fun does not depend on how new or shiny a game is.
fact: Offering a go to someone watching from the side can matter more to them than winning.
fact: A no from parents for one thing can leave time for other things to grow.
exercise: Why did Zara say she «just didn't like games»? | To hide feeling left out without a console | Because she had played them all already | Because her parents banned talking about games | Because she was better at games than everyone
exercise: What did Ruby do that helped Zara? | Asked her to explain her favourite board game | Lent her a console to take home for the weekend | Told the table to stop talking at lunch | Bought her the new game as a present
exercise: What happened after Zara explained her board game? | Half the table played it at break | Everyone laughed at her for liking it | The teacher banned board games | Zara stopped coming to lunch
exercise: How did Kai first feel about «no console until twelve»? | That it was unfair | That it was a fair and sensible rule for a family | That he did not care about it at all | That he would buy one secretly with his savings
exercise: What grew in Kai's spare time? | His drawing and football, and he designed the badge | His anger, until he ran away from home to his gran's | Nothing, he was bored for two years | His collection of console magazines
exercise: Why did Ollie miss the Monday jokes? | He had no phone, so he was not in the group chat | He was always late for school on Monday mornings | He did not understand jokes at all | He had left the group chat on purpose because it was boring
exercise: How did Priya help Ollie? | Told him the funniest bits at break | Gave him her own phone to keep | Started a new chat without the others | Told the others to stop joking
exercise: Theo said Zara couldn't play because she didn't know the game. What did Mia do? | Taught her in five minutes | Agreed and sent Zara home | Told Theo to leave instead | Let Zara watch only
exercise: Why did Noah's lie make things worse? | He could not answer questions about the game | Nobody believed anything he said ever again | The lie got him banned from games | He had to buy the game to prove it
exercise: What happened when Noah told Jayden the truth? | Jayden invited him to play it for real | Jayden told the whole class that he lied | Jayden stopped being his friend | Jayden said he should lie better
exercise: How did they make the sixth kid feel better about the floor? | Cushions and the name «VIP section» | A rule that the floor kid goes home first | A bigger sofa bought by Mia's mum | A turn chart that skipped the floor kid
exercise: Ruby could only play on Saturdays. What did Mia do? | Saved the best challenge for Saturdays | Moved all the games to Wednesdays | Told Ruby to ask her dad again for a console | Played without Ruby every time
exercise: Why did the kids end up loving Leo's old console? | The old two-player games were hilarious | They wanted to laugh at the old blocky graphics | Leo paid them to come round | It was secretly a new console
exercise: How did Zara feel when Jayden handed her his controller? | Seen and a bit shy | Embarrassed and angry | Sure that she would win | Bored because she had to play
exercise: Why did Zara want to come again even after losing badly? | Being invited mattered more than winning | She thought she would win next time if she practised | Jayden promised to let her win | Her mum made her go
exercise: Which is the most helpful way to include a friend with no console? | Do things they like sometimes too | Only talk about games when they leave | Feel sorry for them out loud | Tell them to save up and buy one
test: «zara u don't even play games, ur so boring». What's true? | Not having a console doesn't make you boring | Kids without consoles have no hobbies | Zara should pretend to play games | Boring people don't like games
test: «lol Ollie wasn't in the chat, he missed the meme». Kind move? | Tell him the funny bits | Laugh even harder so he feels left out | Tell him to go and get a phone | Start a chat called No Ollie
test: Noah: «yeah I've got it, level 40». Jayden: «what's the boss on level 40?» Noah goes red. Best for Noah now? | Tell the truth; it's easier than holding a lie | Make up a boss fight and hope nobody checks | Say Jayden's the liar | Never talk about games again
test: Theo: «nah, she can't play, she's a noob». What's the lesson's answer? | Everyone was a noob once; teach her | Noobs should only watch the real players | She should practise alone first | Theo's right, games are for pros
test: «six of us, sofa fits five, someone's on the floor again». What made the floor the best seat? | Cushions and calling it the VIP section | Making the youngest kid sit on the floor always | A rule the floor kid plays last | Turning the telly towards the sofa
test: «blocky graphics lmao, that's your uncle's ancient thing??» What did the kids find out? | The old two-player games were brilliant fun | Old consoles always break in a week | Nobody can play old games now | Old games are only fun for uncles
test: Kai: «no console till I'm TWELVE?? my life is over». What happened to Kai, really? | He got great at drawing and football | His life was boring and empty for two years | He fell out with everyone in his class | He saved up and secretly bought one himself
test: Ruby only gets Saturdays. The group plans the big boss battle for Wednesday. Better plan? | Do the big battle on Saturday when Ruby's there | Do it Wednesday, Ruby can just watch a video of it after | Cancel all battles forever | Let Ruby do it alone later
test: Zara's on the edge of the room watching, says nothing. «want a go?» — why does it matter? | Being invited means more than winning | She'll win and show everyone up | It's the rule that guests play first | She'll stop watching if she loses
test: Teacher: «Why might a pupil say they don't like games when they don't own a console?» | To hide feeling left out | Because games are bad for you | Because they've already finished every game | To make the teacher happy
test: Lunch table, all game talk. Kai's a no-console kid. «what do YOU like then?» Best result? | Kai shares and joins in | Kai has to leave the table and sit somewhere else | Kai lies about owning a game | The table stops talking about games
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a pupil without a console asserted a dislike of games. | A desire to conceal the feeling of being excluded | A genuine and lifelong aversion to all play | An instruction from the school to that effect | A wish to impress the teaching staff
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a falsehood told in order to avoid exclusion from a group? | It is liable to increase the teller's sense of isolation | It reliably secures acceptance by the group | It is harmless provided it is never discovered | It is required of all new members of a group
exam: It is given that a member of a class lacks access to a messaging group. Select the conduct consistent with the guidance of this lesson. | The relating in person of what the member has missed | The creation of a second group excluding the member | The ridicule of the member's lack of a telephone | The avoidance of the member during breaks
exam: Whereas a newcomer is unacquainted with a game, state the response most consistent with the established facts. | Instruction of the newcomer, all experts having once been beginners | Exclusion of the newcomer until proficiency is attained | Permanent assignment of the newcomer to the role of observer | Requirement that the newcomer purchase the game
